==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{term|akr|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{m|gem-pro|*akraz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|is}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₂éǵros||field}} whence also the Danish {{term|ager|lang=da}}, Faroese {{term|akur|lang=fo}}, Swedish {{term|åker|lang=sv}} and Norwegian {{term|åker|lang=no}}.

Germanic cognates include Old English {{term|æcer|lang=ang}} (English {{term|acre|lang=en}}), Old Frisian {{term|ekker|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|akkar|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|akker|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|ackar|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Acker|lang=de}}) and Gothic {{term|𐌰𐌺𐍂𐍃|lang=got}}. Other cognates include Ancient Greek {{term|ἀγρός||lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}, Mycenaean Greek {{term|𐀀𐀒𐀫|lang=gmy}}, Latin {{term|ager|lang=la}} (Spanish {{term|agerio|lang=es}} and {{term|agreste|lang=es}}), Umbrian {{m|xum|ager}}, Old Armenian {{term|արտ|lang=xcl}} and Sanskrit {{term|अज्र|tr=ájra|lang=sa|sc=Deva}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈaːk(ʰ)ʏr]|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|lang=is|aːkʏr}}

